{
  "slug": "oxygen-transport",
  "title": "How Your Blood Carries Oxygen",
  "category": "Physiology",
  "audience": "Pre-nursing and A&P students",
  "learningObjectives": [
    "Explain how erythrocyte structure supports gas transport",
    "Describe oxygen loading in the lungs and unloading in tissues, including the Bohr effect",
    "Recognize expected findings in a client with anemia"
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "The bone marrow produces more than 2 million red blood cells per second.",
      "source": "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e, §18.3 Erythrocytes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Erythrocytes are biconcave discs that lack a nucleus; the shape increases surface area for gas exchange and flexibility to pass through narrow capillaries.",
      "source": "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e, §18.3 Erythrocytes"
    },
    {
      "text": "An erythrocyte contains about 300 million hemoglobin molecules.",
      "source": "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e, §18.3 Erythrocytes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Each hemoglobin has four heme groups, each containing an iron ion that binds one O2; one hemoglobin carries up to four oxygen molecules.",
      "source": "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e, §18.3 Erythrocytes; §22.5 Transport of Gases"
    },
    {
      "text": "Oxygen diffuses from the alveoli into pulmonary capillary blood and binds hemoglobin to form oxyhemoglobin.",
      "source": "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e, §22.4 Gas Exchange; §22.5 Transport of Gases"
    },
    {
      "text": "Oxygenated blood is bright red; deoxygenated blood is dark red, not blue.",
      "source": "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e, §18.3 Erythrocytes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Oxygenated blood returns from the lungs to the left side of the heart, which pumps it through systemic arteries.",
      "source": "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e, §19.1 Heart Anatomy"
    },
    {
      "text": "Increased CO2 and decreased pH reduce hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen, promoting unloading in tissues (Bohr effect).",
      "source": "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e, §22.5 Transport of Gases"
    },
    {
      "text": "Most CO2 is transported as bicarbonate, formed in erythrocytes by carbonic anhydrase; smaller amounts bind hemoglobin or dissolve in plasma. (Exact percentages vary by source, so none are stated.)",
      "source": "OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e, §22.5 Transport of Gases; OpenStax Biology 2e, §39.4"
    },
    {
      "text": "Anemia reduces oxygen-carrying capacity; fatigue and compensatory tachycardia are expected findings.",
      "source": "Hinkle & Cheever, Brunner & Suddarth's Textbook of Medical-Surgical Nursing, Anemia: clinical manifestations"
    }
  ],
  "review": {
    "status": "pending"
  },
